      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The village’s first mill, built in 1765 by James and George Mercer, sits where the historic Aldie Mill now stands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldie%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Aldie’s 108‑foot Little River Turnpike Bridge, a fieldstone arch, is a National Historic Resource and a centerpiece of the Aldie Mill Historic Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.aldieheritage.com/history",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the Civil War, Union cavalry skirmished with Confederate partisan John Singleton Mosby’s rangers right in Aldie’s streets.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aldie%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Oak Hill, the home of President James Monroe, lies just outside Aldie and was built in 1822.",
